**Date:** YYYY-MM-DD
**Tier:** Light | Medium | Deep
**Status:** Draft

## Problem

What is broken, missing, or annoying today. Concrete, not aspirational. If there is a
triggering incident, a support ticket, or a manual workaround being repeated, name it.

## Goals

What this must achieve, as a short list. Each goal should be checkable — someone reading
the finished implementation should be able to say yes or no to each one.

## Non-goals

What is deliberately excluded, each with its reason. This section exists so cut scope does
not creep back in three weeks later. Include everything YAGNI'd during the interview.

- <Thing not being built> — <why not, or what would have to change to justify it>

## Constraints

Existing systems that cannot change, compatibility requirements, deadlines, dependency
limits, operational constraints, code that must not be touched.

## Chosen approach

How it works, in enough detail that someone could start building without asking follow-up
questions.

### Alternatives rejected

For each alternative that was seriously considered: what it was, and the specific reason
it lost. Future readers will propose these again — this section answers them.

- **<Alternative>** — <why it was rejected>

## Components and interfaces

Each unit: what it does, its interface, what it depends on. Boundaries should be clear
enough that units can be built and tested independently.

## Data flow

How data moves through the system. Sources, transformations, destinations, and where state
lives. A diagram is welcome if it beats the prose.

{{DOMAIN_SPECIFIC_SECTIONS}}

## Error handling

What can fail, how each failure is detected, what the system does in response, and what the
user sees. Include partial-failure and retry behaviour where relevant.

## Testing strategy

What gets tested and at what level. Name the cases that actually matter — including the
edge cases surfaced during the interview — rather than asserting that coverage will be
good.

{{VERIFICATION_NOTE}}

## Open questions

Anything genuinely undecided, with who decides and by when. An empty section is the
expected outcome; if this list is long, the spec is not finished.

## Decision log

Decisions made during the design conversation that are not obvious from the sections
above, so they do not get relitigated.

| Decision | Reasoning |
| --- | --- |
| <what was decided> | <why> |
